## Example

Assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ for the supplied gauge datum. Use $M=\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and $c=\hbar=1$. Let the gauge group be the compact group $SU(2)$ on the trivial principal bundle with the product connection, whose global-section potential is $A_\mu=0$. Gauge transformations are smooth maps $u:M\to SU(2)$ acting by $H(x)\mapsto u(x)H(x)$ and $\widetilde H(x)\mapsto\widetilde H(x)u(x)^{-1}$. Take one even Lorentz-scalar complex doublet $H$ in the standard unitary representation. In the complex-algebraic polynomial model, use $G_{\mathrm{alg}}=SO(\eta,\mathbb C)\times SL(2,\mathbb C)$, where $SO(\eta,\mathbb C)=\{L\in GL_4(\mathbb C):L^T\eta L=\eta,\det L=1\}$ acts trivially on these scalar fields, and treat $H\in\mathbb C^2$ and an independent dual variable $\widetilde H\in(\mathbb C^2)^*$ with the standard $SL(2,\mathbb C)$ and dual actions. The physical Lorentz and gauge groups map to $G_{\mathrm{alg}}$ by $(L,u)\mapsto(L,u)$, viewing the real proper orthochronous Lorentz matrix $L$ as a complex matrix and the same $SU(2)$ matrix $u$ as an element of $SL(2,\mathbb C)$; the standard and dual actions restrict to the specified physical actions. Impose $\widetilde H=H^\dagger$ for the real action. Take no flavor symmetry, field weight one, derivative cutoff $r=0$, maximum field degree $q=6$, retained engineering dimension $D_{\max}=6$, loop order $L_{\max}=0$, and a positive coefficient scale $\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}$. For this illustration the declared relation subspace is zero. Then $\mathcal O_2=\widetilde H H$, $\mathcal O_4=(\widetilde H H)^2$, and $\mathcal O_6=(\widetilde H H)^3$ are gauge-invariant scalar polynomials of engineering dimensions $2$, $4$, and $6$. With real dimensionless $C_6$, the term $C_6\mathcal O_6/\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}^2$ has Lagrangian density dimension $4$ and belongs to this dimension-six truncation. For $a,b\in\{1,2\}$, define $\varepsilon_{12}=1$, $\varepsilon_{21}=-1$, and $\varepsilon_{11}=\varepsilon_{22}=0$; then the antisymmetric polynomial $\varepsilon_{ab}H^aH^b$ vanishes because the even field components commute. This is a finite scalar-sector illustration, not a complete $SU(2)$ EFT basis or a claim that these coefficients describe nature.

## Example references

[C. P. Burgess, Introduction to Effective Field Theory, §§3.1–3.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0701053)

[David B. Kaplan, Five lectures on effective field theory, §1.2 and §§2.4.1–2.4.2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/nucl-th/0510023)

