+
+## Example
+
+Assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ for the supplied gauge datum. Use $M=\mathbb R^{1,3}$, $x^0=ct$, $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and $c=\hbar=1$. Let $\phi\in C_c^\infty(M,\mathbb R)$ be an even real Lorentz-scalar of engineering weight one, a singlet of a fixed $SU(2)$ gauge datum on the trivial bundle with the product connection, and odd under the global group $\mathbb Z_2=\{1,-1\}$ acting by $\phi\mapsto-\phi$. The finite-jet module is the complexification of this real scalar field with conjugation fixing $\phi$. For the finite-jet quotient use $G_{\mathrm{alg}}=SO(\eta,\mathbb C)\times SL(2,\mathbb C)\times\mu_2$, where $SO(\eta,\mathbb C)=\{L\in GL_4(\mathbb C):L^T\eta L=\eta,\det L=1\}$ and $\mu_2=\{z\in\mathbb C:z^2=1\}$; the Lorentz factor acts on derivative indices, $SL(2,\mathbb C)$ acts trivially on $\phi$, and $\mu_2$ acts by sign. At the finite field-representation level, the physical group $SO^+(1,3)\times SU(2)\times\mathbb Z_2$ maps to $G_{\mathrm{alg}}$ by $(L,u,\sigma)\mapsto(L,I_2,\sigma)$, where $L$ is included as the same real matrix in $SO(\eta,\mathbb C)$, the gauge factor maps trivially, and $\sigma=\pm1$ is included as the same element of $\mu_2$. Thus each scalar jet transforms by the Lorentz action on its derivative indices, trivially under $SU(2)$, and by sign under $\mathbb Z_2$. Let $m\ge0$ be a constant $\mathbb Z_2$-invariant mass spurion of weight one, let $\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}>0$ have weight one, and let $C\in\mathbb R$ be dimensionless. Define $\Box=\eta^{\mu\nu}\partial_\mu\partial_\nu$ and adopt the base action
+
+$$S_0[\phi]=-\frac12\int_M\bigl(\partial_\mu\phi\,\partial^\mu\phi+m^2\phi^2\bigr)\,d^4x$$
+
+and add the dimension-six correction
+
+$$S_6[\phi]=\frac{C}{\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}^2}\int_M\phi^2\partial_\mu\phi\,\partial^\mu\phi\,d^4x.$$
+
+For compactly supported $h$, define the leading Euler derivative by $DS_0[\phi](h)=\int_Mh\mathcal E_0(\phi)\,d^4x$; the calculation below verifies $\mathcal E_0(\phi)=(\Box-m^2)\phi$. Take derivative cutoff $r=2$, maximum field degree $q=4$, retained engineering dimension $D_{\max}=6$, and loop order $L_{\max}=0$. Let the exact declared dimension-six relation subspace be the $G_{\mathrm{alg}}$-invariant span of $\partial_\mu(\phi^3\partial^\mu\phi)$ and $\phi^3\mathcal E_0(\phi)$. All fields and variations have compact support, so the displayed integrations by parts have no boundary term. Retain terms through formal order $\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}^{-2}$, with $O(\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}^{-4})$ denoting terms at least quadratic in the formal bookkeeping parameter $\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}^{-2}$, not a numerical error bound. Then $\phi^2\partial_\mu\phi\,\partial^\mu\phi$ represents $-\frac{m^2}{3}\phi^4$ in this quotient, and the local field substitution $\phi_{\mathrm{old}}=\phi+\frac{C}{3\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}^2}\phi^3$ changes $S_0+S_6$ to $S_0-\frac{Cm^2}{3\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}^2}\int_M\phi^4\,d^4x+O(\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}^{-4})$. This is an action-level statement at the retained order only.
+
+## Example references
+
+[C. P. Burgess, Introduction to Effective Field Theory, §2.1, equations (21)–(26)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0701053)
+
+[Juan Carlos Criado and Manuel Pérez-Victoria, Field redefinitions in effective theories at higher orders, §§3 and 5.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1811.09413)
